# Introduction

Reaching the end of a non-void, non-main, and non-coroutine function without returning is undefined behavior in C and
C++. Historically, in C this partly originated from pre-standard C defaulting return types to `int` and lacking `void`.
As C and C++ evolved, leaving non-returning paths as unreachable compilers to assume such code paths are unreachable and
optimize accordingly, e.g. the following results in only one comparison:
```cpp
enum class E { A, B };

std::string_view to_sv(E e) {
    if(e == E::A) {
        return "A";
    } else if(e == E::B) {
        return "B";
    }
}
```
```asm
to_sv(E):
        test    edi, edi
        lea     rax, [rip + .L.str]
        lea     rdx, [rip + .L.str.1]
        cmove   rdx, rax
        mov     eax, 1
        ret
```

This is, however, a footgun in C++ and a safety issue. For example, if `C` is added to the enum in the above code it
would be easy to inadvertently forget to update `to_sv` for this new case, especially if `to_sv`'s definition is in a
separate file. On an optimized build calling `to_sv(E::C)` typically results in buggy behavior due to an incorrect
string being returned. In a debug build this typically crashes.

Most widely used languages do not allow such footguns and issue compile-time errors for such code.

Currently all major C++ compilers issue warnings for missing returns but the developer must opt-in to this being treated
as a critical error. While many projects do opt-in to treating these warnings as errors, many don't and some programmers
are in the habit of ignoring warnings. Given that C++ now has a standard means to explicitly mark code paths as
unreachable, namely `std::unreachable`, we propose that missing returns in control flow paths be ill-formed in favor
of explicitly marking non-returning paths as unreachable.

# Current Implementation Behavior

GCC, Clang, MSVC, ICX, and EDG currently warn on missing returns by default:
[https://godbolt.org/z/5M3xs88W6](https://godbolt.org/z/5M3xs88W6).

